// Generated by examples/ratelimit_fixture.py from ratelimit_oracle.json —
// do not edit by hand.
//
// What limits 5.8.0 and slowapi 0.1.10 did, captured on CPython
// 3.14.7 from the backend's virtualenv with `time.time` frozen:
//
// * `parse_*`: 63 strings handed to `limits.parse_many` — each item
//   as `amount|multiples|granularity|repr|expiry|key_for(ip, path)`, one
//   per line, or the `ValueError`.
// * `memory_*`: 63 calls on `FixedWindowRateLimiter(MemoryStorage())`,
//   9 scripts each on a fresh storage: the call, the time
//   in milliseconds, the limit string, the identifiers (one per line), the
//   cost, and what came back — `true`/`false`, or `reset_ms,remaining`.
// * `redis_*`: 16 calls on the same strategy over `RedisStorage`
//   (redis-py 6.4.0, redis-server 8.0.2), with every
//   command written and every reply read, back to back. redis-py's own
//   `CLIENT SETINFO` handshake is left out.
// * `app_*`: 103 requests through slowapi in front of FastAPI, in
//   4 applications (`examples/ratelimit_oracle.py` has
//   them): the client address, method, path, and time; the status, body,
//   and rate-limit headers (`name: value` lines, repeated headers joined
//   with `, `); and `request.state.view_rate_limit` with its window stats.

module ratelimit::fixture;

